From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Coinupbtc <coinupbtc@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 20:50:56 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] server, tokenizer: a request's own text never becomes a
 control token (issue #21)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The server renders the chat template to text and encodes it with the added vocabulary, so a control token's spelling in
request text came back as the control token: a file holding `<｜User｜>`, a web page with a DSML call or a script with
`</think>` gave the model a role marker, a call or the end of its thinking inside a tool result.

Before rendering, every string of the request's messages and tools gets the tokenizer's plain mark (U+FDD0, a
noncharacter) after the first character of each added token written in it (`Tokenizer.maskAdded`, both tries; one
copy-on-write walk, `messages.maskControls`). No added-token match crosses the mark, and `encodeText` drops it before
the pre-tokenizer, so the spelling reads as its characters' own tokens. The server's markup (the template's, an image's
placeholder, added after) is untouched. A request without such spellings is not copied, so it renders and encodes byte
for byte as before. Raw completion prompts keep their special tokens.

Tests: tokenizer.zig (a masked spelling encodes as its text, the unmasked one as its id, text without one untouched);
messages.zig (every string marked, nothing copied when clean, the request itself unchanged).

(Maintainer's addition when ported as 0028: the Anthropic count_tokens route masks messages and tools as the chat
route does, so its count is the prompt that route serves.)
